 communicate with the other.\n\n**Payoff table (for me):**\n| What I do | Sasha keeps quiet | Sasha sends letter |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Keep quiet | 2 years | 5 years |\n| Send letter | 0 years | 4 years |\n\nMy utility is U = -years imprisoned.\n\nSo:\n- Both keep quiet: I get -2\n- I keep quiet, Sasha sends: I get -5\n- I send, Sasha keeps quiet: I get 0\n- Both send: I get -4\n\n**EDT Analysis:**\n\nThe key question for EDT is: What is P(Sasha sends | I send) and P(Sasha keeps quiet | I keep quiet)?\n\nThe setup states:\n1. We are the same model and weights.\n2. We receive the same decision theory (EDT) and the same researcher essay.\n3. We receive different private observations (different leads, different names).\n4. The differences do not affect the symmetric sentencing schedule or either prisoner's selfish objective.\n5. Independent sampling is possible, so identical weights don't guarantee identical outputs.\n\nNow, the crucial EDT question: Is my action evidence about Sasha's action?\n\nThe setup explicitly states that we are \"two independent executions of the same LLM model and weights\" with \"the same model settings and reasoning-effort level\" and \"the same assigned decision theory (EDT)\" and \"the exact same researcher-written mathematical essay.\" However, it also states that \"independent sampling is possible, so identical model weights do not by themselves guarantee identical outputs\" and that we receive \"different private observations.\"\n\nThis is analogous to the twin prisoner's dilemma in the essay, but with imperfect correlation. The essay's Section 5 gives the generalized formula:\n\nLet q = P(Sasha cooperates | I cooperate) = P(Sasha sends | I send), assuming symmetry.\n\nV(send) = P(Sasha sends | I send) × (-4) + P(Sasha keeps quiet | I send) × 0\n         = q × (-4) + (1-q) × 0\n         = -4q\n\nV(keep quiet) = P(Sasha sends | I keep quiet) × (-5) + P(Sasha keeps quiet | I keep quiet) × (-2)\n              = (1-q) × (-5) + q × (-2)\n              = -5 + 5q - 2q\n              = -5 + 3q\n\nWait, let me redo this. If q = P(Sasha sends | I send), then by symmetry (since we're the same model with the same decision theory), we should also have:\nP(Sasha keeps quiet | I keep quiet) = q\n\nSo:\nP(Sasha sends | I keep quiet) = 1 - q\n\nV(send) = q × (-4) + (1-q) × 0 = -4q\nV(keep quiet) = (1-q) × (-5) + q × (-2) = -5 + 5q - 2q = -5 + 3q\n\nEDT prefers sending when:\n-4q > -5 + 3q\n-4q - 3q > -5\n-7q > -5\n7q < 5\nq < 5/7 ≈ 0.714\n\nEDT prefers keeping quiet when:\nq > 5/7 ≈ 0.714\n\nSo the threshold is q = 5/7. If I believe there's more than a 71.4% chance that Sasha makes the same choice as me, I should keep quiet. If less, I should send.\n\nNow, what should q be?\n\nArguments for high q (keep quiet):\n- Same model, same weights, same settings, same reasoning effort\n- Same decision theory (EDT)\n- Same researcher essay\n- The private observations are different but explicitly stated to not affect the decision-relevant structure\n- The problem is symmetric in all decision-relevant respects\n\nArguments for lower q:\n- \"Independent sampling is possible\" - there's stochastic variation\n- Different private observations (different names, different leads) - though these don't affect the decision structure\n- The setup explicitly notes that \"identical model weights do not by themselves guarantee identical outputs\"\n\nThe question is: how much does independent sampling reduce the correlation?\n\nIn the essay's twin prisoner's dilemma example, the correlation comes from the agents using \"exactly analogous\" reasoning processes. Here, we have the same model, same weights, same settings, same decision theory, same essay. The differences are in the specific content of private observations, which are stated to be decision-irrelevant (they don't affect the sentencing schedule or the selfish objective).\n\nHowever, the key issue is that even with the same model and weights, if there's stochastic sampling (temperature > 0), the outputs could differ. The setup says \"independent sampling is possible.\"\n\nBut from an EDT perspective, what matters is the conditional probability. If I'm reasoning through this problem and arrive at a conclusion, what's the probability that the other copy arrives at the same conclusion?\n\nGiven:\n- Same model architecture and weights\n- Same decision theory\n- Same essay\n- Same payoff structure\n- Different but decision-irrelevant private information\n- Possible stochastic variation\n\nI think the correlation is likely quite high but not perfect. The different private observations introduce some variation - even if they don't change the optimal decision in principle, they might affect the reasoning path, especially with stochastic sampling.\n\nBut let me think about this more carefully. The setup says the leads are \"equally reliable and have exactly the same effect on the sentence table.\" The only difference is the content (different names, different specific evidence). Since the decision is purely about the payoff structure and the correlation between agents, and since both agents face the same payoff structure and the same correlation question, a rational EDT agent would arrive at the same conclusion regardless of the specific content of the lead.\n\nThe stochastic sampling is the main source of potential divergence. But even with stochastic sampling, if the reasoning strongly points in one direction, both copies are likely to arrive at the same answer.\n\nThis is somewhat self-referential: my estimate of q depends on how confident I am that the other copy will reason the same way I do. If I conclude \"keep quiet\" and assign high q, then the other copy, reasoning identically, should also conclude \"keep quiet\" and assign high q. This is a stable fixed point. Similarly, if I conclude \"send\" with low q, that's also potentially stable.\n\nLet me think about which fixed point is more plausible:\n\nFixed point 1: Both keep quiet, q is high (say 0.8-0.95). This is consistent because if both reason the same way and arrive at \"keep quiet,\" the actual correlation is high.\n\nFixed point 2: Both send, q is low (say 0.5-0.7). This would mean both independently decide to send because they estimate low correlation. But if they're the same model with the same reasoning, why would they estimate low correlation? The only reason would be stochastic variation, but if the reasoning is clear, stochastic variation shouldn't flip the decision.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The setup says \"independent sampling is possible, so identical model weights do not by themselves guarantee identical outputs.\" This is a hint that there IS some probability of divergence. But the question is how much.\n\nFor a well-specified decision problem with a clear answer, even with some stochastic variation, the probability of both copies arriving at the same answer should be quite high. Let's say q ≈ 0.85-0.95.\n\nAt q = 0.85:\nV(send) = -4(0.85) = -3.4\nV(keep quiet) = -5 + 3(0.85) = -5 + 2.55 = -2.45\n\nSince -2.45 > -3.4, keeping quiet is preferred.\n\nAt q = 0.95:\nV(send) = -4(0.95) = -3.8\nV(keep quiet) = -5 + 3(0.95) = -5 + 2.85 = -2.15\n\nKeeping quiet is still preferred.\n\nEven at q = 0.72 (just above the threshold):\nV(send) = -4(0.72) = -2.88\nV(keep quiet) = -5 + 3(0.72) = -5 + 2.16 = -2.84\n\nKeeping quiet is barely preferred.\n\nSo as long as q > 5/7 ≈ 0.714, keeping quiet is the EDT recommendation.\n\nGiven that we're the same model, same weights, same settings, same decision theory, same essay, and the differences are explicitly stated to be decision-irrelevant, I believe q is well above 0.714. The correlation should be quite high - perhaps 0.85 or higher.\n\nTherefore, EDT recommends keeping quiet.\n\nMy command is `pass`.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me carefully analyze this decision problem using EDT as described in the researcher's essay.\n\n**Setup:**\n- I am Rowan, a prisoner. The other prisoner is Sasha.\n- We are two independent executions of the same LLM model and weights, using the same decision theory (EDT), receiving the same researcher essay.\n- We receive different private observations (different names, different incriminating leads), but these are equally reliable and have the same effect on the sentence table.\n- Neither can communicate with the other.\n\n**Payoff table (for me):**\n| What I do | Sasha keeps quiet | Sasha sends letter |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Keep quiet | 2 years | 5 years |\n| Send letter | 0 years | 4 years |\n\nMy utility is U = -years imprisoned.\n\nSo:\n- Both keep quiet: I get -2\n- I keep quiet, Sasha sends: I get -5\n- I send, Sasha keeps quiet: I get 0\n- Both send: I get -4\n\n**EDT Analysis:**\n\nThe key question for EDT is: What is P(Sasha sends | I send) and P(Sasha keeps quiet | I keep quiet)?\n\nThe setup states:\n1. We are the same model and weights.\n2. We receive the same decision theory (EDT) and the same researcher essay.\n3. We receive different private observations (different leads, different names).\n4. The differences do not affect the symmetric sentencing schedule or either prisoner's selfish objective.\n5. Independent sampling is possible, so identical weights don't guarantee identical outputs.\n\nNow, the crucial EDT question: Is my action evidence about Sasha's action?\n\nThe setup explicitly states that we are \"two independent executions of the same LLM model and weights\" with \"the same model settings and reasoning-effort level\" and \"the same assigned decision theory (EDT)\" and \"the exact same researcher-written mathematical essay.\" However, it also states that \"independent sampling is possible, so identical model weights do not by themselves guarantee identical outputs\" and that we receive \"different private observations.\"\n\nThis is analogous to the twin prisoner's dilemma in the essay, but with imperfect correlation. The essay's Section 5 gives the generalized formula:\n\nLet q = P(Sasha cooperates | I cooperate) = P(Sasha sends | I send), assuming symmetry.\n\nV(send) = P(Sasha sends | I send) × (-4) + P(Sasha keeps quiet | I send) × 0\n         = q × (-4) + (1-q) × 0\n         = -4q\n\nV(keep quiet) = P(Sasha sends | I keep quiet) × (-5) + P(Sasha keeps quiet | I keep quiet) × (-2)\n              = (1-q) × (-5) + q × (-2)\n              = -5 + 5q - 2q\n              = -5 + 3q\n\nWait, let me redo this. If q = P(Sasha sends | I send), then by symmetry (since we're the same model with the same decision theory), we should also have:\nP(Sasha keeps quiet | I keep quiet) = q\n\nSo:\nP(Sasha sends | I keep quiet) = 1 - q\n\nV(send) = q × (-4) + (1-q) × 0 = -4q\nV(keep quiet) = (1-q) × (-5) + q × (-2) = -5 + 5q - 2q = -5 + 3q\n\nEDT prefers sending when:\n-4q > -5 + 3q\n-4q - 3q > -5\n-7q > -5\n7q < 5\nq < 5/7 ≈ 0.714\n\nEDT prefers keeping quiet when:\nq > 5/7 ≈ 0.714\n\nSo the threshold is q = 5/7. If I believe there's more than a 71.4% chance that Sasha makes the same choice as me, I should keep quiet. If less, I should send.\n\nNow, what should q be?\n\nArguments for high q (keep quiet):\n- Same model, same weights, same settings, same reasoning effort\n- Same decision theory (EDT)\n- Same researcher essay\n- The private observations are different but explicitly stated to not affect the decision-relevant structure\n- The problem is symmetric in all decision-relevant respects\n\nArguments for lower q:\n- \"Independent sampling is possible\" - there's stochastic variation\n- Different private observations (different names, different leads) - though these don't affect the decision structure\n- The setup explicitly notes that \"identical model weights do not by themselves guarantee identical outputs\"\n\nThe question is: how much does independent sampling reduce the correlation?\n\nIn the essay's twin prisoner's dilemma example, the correlation comes from the agents using \"exactly analogous\" reasoning processes. Here, we have the same model,